R is an open-source programming language and environment designed primarily for statistical computing, data analysis, and data visualization.
Development of R began in the early 1990s, and the language has since developed a large ecosystem of packages for statistics, epidemiology, data science, and scientific research.



History of R




The Comprehensive R Archive Network (CRAN) is the primary repository for R packages and distributions of R.
CRAN provides access to packages that extend R for specialized tasks such as:

statistical modeling;
data manipulation;
visualization;
epidemiology;
machine learning;
reporting and reproducible research.




R is particularly well suited to analytical and statistical workflows.
Its ecosystem includes tools for:

data manipulation;
exploratory data analysis;
statistical modeling;
visualization;
reproducible reporting.



The R ecosystem includes ggplot2, a widely used system for declarative data visualization based on the Grammar of Graphics.




R was designed primarily around statistical computing and data analysis, whereas Python is a general-purpose programming language with applications extending well beyond data science.
See R and Python for a comparison.

